title: Tackle Tool Call API Surface
status: implemented
created: 2026-10-29
author: adrian
context: "Tackle addresses tools by canonical invokable names: `mcp.<server>.<tool>`, `prompt.<name>`, `agent.<actor>`, and `client.<surface>.<method>`. The registry currently emits those names directly to model providers through `Registry::model_tools` and `Registry::manifest`, and the OpenAI chat-completions provider passes them to rig unchanged. Strict providers reject names containing dots: OpenAI and Anthropic require `^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]{1,64}$`, DeepSeek documents `^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]+$`, Gemini allows dots but requires a leading letter or underscore, Meta allows at most one dot, and Cohere disallows a leading digit. A dotted MCP name such as `mcp.github.search_issues` therefore fails before the model runs. A request-only Switchboard mutation cannot fix the response half because the model returns the rewritten name. This ADR refines FR-009 of `2026-09-17-tackle`, which assumed the model sees canonical namespaced names. Switchboard proxy changes are out of scope."
problem: Canonical invokable names are not valid tool names on several model surfaces, and the registry has no surface-aware encoding, decoding, warning, or configuration. Tool use fails on strict providers, and there is no traceability from a wire name back to its canonical invokable.
goals[6]:
  - Keep canonical invokable names in the registry, storage, permission pipeline, and dispatch
  - Render wire-safe tool names per API surface
  - Decode model tool calls back to canonical names before dispatch
  - Keep rendered names human-readable and troubleshooting-friendly
  - Warn when a rendered name loses information
  - Configure naming per endpoint and per model, with sensible wire-format defaults
nonGoals[6]:
  - Switchboard proxy changes or response rewriting
  - Changing the canonical `mcp.<server>.<tool>` naming convention
  - Runtime discovery of provider naming rules
  - Persisting wire names in sessions or telemetry
  - Encoding `tool_choice` (not present in `ModelRequest` today)
  - Translating names for clients other than Tackle
requirements[12]{id,title,slug,description}:
  FR-001,Canonical And Wire Separation,canonical-and-wire-separation,"The registry, session storage, permission pipeline, and dispatch use `InvokableName` exclusively. `WireToolName` exists only at the model surface; no wire name is persisted or used for dispatch."
  FR-002,Tool Name Scheme,tool-name-scheme,"`invokables/tool_name.rs` defines `ToolNameScheme` with `encode(InvokableName) -> WireToolName` and `decode(WireToolName) -> Option<InvokableName>`, plus implementations for `strict`, `gemini`, `meta`, `cohere`, and `custom`. The scheme and map live beside the registry; provider modules consume them and do not implement naming rules."
  FR-003,Readable Encoding,readable-encoding,"Encoding replaces each namespace dot with `__`, replaces every other character outside the scheme's allowed set with `_`, preserves readable segments, and enforces the scheme's maximum length. On collision or truncation, append `_` plus the first 8 hex characters of SHA-256 of the canonical name. The result is deterministic for a canonical name and scheme."
  FR-004,Tool Name Map,tool-name-map,"`ToolNameMap` is per request. It maps canonical names from the current model-invokable set and from historical tool calls and results to wire names. Model tool calls are decoded only against the current model-invokable set; a wire name outside that set passes through unchanged to the existing unknown-invokable error path."
  FR-005,Registry Render Entry Point,registry-render-entry-point,"`Registry::render_tools(scheme)` returns `RenderedTools { tools, manifest, names }`. Tools and manifest contain wire names; `names` is the `ToolNameMap`. The registry stores no scheme. Direct invocation and ACP command advertisement continue to use canonical names."
  FR-006,Provider Encode And Decode,provider-encode-and-decode,"The model provider encodes historical tool call and result names with `names`, and decodes every model tool call before returning `ModelResponse`. Tool definitions arrive from `Registry::render_tools` already rendered. `ModelResponse.tool_calls[].name` is always canonical."
  FR-007,Wire-Named Manifest,wire-named-manifest,"The system prompt invokable manifest is rendered through the same `ToolNameMap` as the tool definitions, so the model sees exactly the wire names it can call. Canonical names do not appear in model-visible text."
  FR-008,Endpoint And Model Configuration,endpoint-and-model-configuration,"Configuration adds `tool_names` at the endpoint and per model: `[endpoints.<name>.tool_names]` and `[endpoints.<name>.models.<model>.tool_names]`, where the model key is a quoted TOML key. Each table accepts `scheme`, optional `max_length`, and optional `pattern`. The existing `models = [...]` list form remains valid. Precedence is model over endpoint over wire-format default."
  FR-009,Surface Defaults,surface-defaults,"Wire-format defaults are `strict` for `openai-chat-completions` and `anthropic-messages`, `gemini` for `google-generate-content` when implemented, and `strict` for unknown formats. `strict` allows `[A-Za-z0-9_]` with a maximum of 64 characters and uses `__` for namespace boundaries."
  FR-010,Custom Scheme And Pattern,custom-scheme-and-pattern,"`scheme = custom` requires `pattern` and may set `max_length`. `pattern` without `custom` is a configuration error. Selecting an unimplemented surface scheme, such as `gemini` before that provider exists, is a configuration error."
  FR-011,Lossy Rewrite Warnings,lossy-rewrite-warnings,"A rewrite that loses information, meaning truncation or a hash suffix, emits `tracing::warn!()` with the canonical name, wire name, endpoint, model, and surface. Routine dot-to-`__` and invalid-character-to-`_` rewrites are reversible through the map and do not warn. Warnings are deduplicated per endpoint, model, and canonical name."
  FR-012,No Silent Drops,no-silent-drops,"If no unique valid wire name fits within the maximum length, the request fails with a precise error naming the canonical name, endpoint, model, and surface. Tools are never silently dropped. An empty canonical name also fails."
nonFunctional[7]{id,title,slug,description}:
  NFR-001,Determinism,determinism,"Given the same canonical names, scheme, and tool set, encoding produces the same wire names. Encoding uses no randomness and no ambient state."
  NFR-002,Request-Local State,request-local-state,"The `ToolNameMap` is built for one model request and discarded with it. No cross-request or process-global naming state exists."
  NFR-003,Backward Compatibility,backward-compatibility,"Existing canonical sessions, stored tool calls, and configurations remain valid. The `models = [...]` list form parses and behaves as models with default options."
  NFR-004,Observability,observability,"Lossy rewrites log the canonical name, wire name, endpoint, model, and surface. Logs contain no credentials or tool arguments."
  NFR-005,Performance,performance,"Encoding and decoding are linear in the number of tools plus history entries. They add no network calls and no additional model requests."
  NFR-006,Testability,testability,"Unit tests cover every scheme and `ToolNameMap`; provider integration tests cover encode, decode, and history rewrite using recorded fixtures and no live endpoints."
  NFR-007,Dispatch Safety,dispatch-safety,"Only canonical names resolve in the registry. A decoded model call cannot execute an invokable that is not in the current model-invokable set."
acceptance[11]{id,title,slug,description}:
  AC-001,Strict Provider Tool Call,strict-provider-tool-call,"Against an endpoint using `strict`, a dotted MCP tool is offered under a safe wire name, the model calls that name, the provider decodes it, and the canonical tool executes."
  AC-002,Canonical Persistence,canonical-persistence,"A completed tool call stores only the canonical `InvokableName` in the session DAG and telemetry."
  AC-003,Manifest Matches Tools,manifest-matches-tools,"Every wire name in the system prompt manifest appears in the tool definitions, and every tool definition appears in the manifest."
  AC-004,Readable Default,readable-default,"With no collision, `mcp.github.search_issues` renders as `mcp__github__search_issues`."
  AC-005,Collision Handling,collision-handling,"Two canonical names that sanitize to the same readable base receive distinct hash suffixes and distinct wire names."
  AC-006,Length And Truncation,length-and-truncation,"A canonical name longer than the configured maximum is truncated to a readable prefix plus a hash suffix, remains within the limit, and emits a warning."
  AC-007,Configuration Precedence,configuration-precedence,"A per-model override wins over an endpoint override, which wins over the wire-format default."
  AC-008,List Compatibility,list-compatibility,"A configuration using `models = [...]` parses and behaves identically to the table form with default options."
  AC-009,Unknown Call Passthrough,unknown-call-passthrough,"A model call whose wire name is not in the current model-invokable set passes through unchanged and produces the existing unknown-invokable error."
  AC-010,Impossible Name Fails,impossible-name-fails,"When no unique valid wire name fits the configured limit, the request fails with a precise error and no tool is silently dropped."
  AC-011,Lossy Warning Only,lossy-warning-only,"Routine namespace and character rewrites produce no warning; truncation and hash suffixes produce exactly one deduplicated warning per endpoint, model, and canonical name."
edgeCases[10]{id,title,slug,description}:
  EC-001,Ambiguous Underscores,ambiguous-underscores,"Canonical components may contain single or double underscores. The per-request map resolves the ambiguity; decoding never splits the wire name on `__` alone."
  EC-002,Quoted Model Keys,quoted-model-keys,"Model names containing slashes or dots are written as quoted TOML keys, for example `[endpoints.switchboard.models.'meta/llama-4']`."
  EC-003,Empty Name,empty-name,"An empty canonical name fails the request with a precise error rather than producing an empty wire name."
  EC-004,Limit Too Small,limit-too-small,"A configured `max_length` too small to fit a readable prefix and hash suffix fails the request with a precise error."
  EC-005,Historical Tool Not Current,historical-tool-not-current,"A historical tool call whose canonical name is not in the current tool set is still encoded for history, but a new model call to that wire name passes through and hits the unknown-invokable path."
  EC-006,Model Returns Canonical Name,model-returns-canonical-name,"A model call that already matches a canonical name but not a current wire name is treated as unknown and passes through unchanged."
  EC-007,Invalid Custom Pattern,invalid-custom-pattern,"An invalid `pattern` is rejected at configuration load with the endpoint and model named."
  EC-008,Scheme And Pattern Conflict,scheme-and-pattern-conflict,"Setting `pattern` without `scheme = custom` is rejected at configuration load."
  EC-009,Unimplemented Surface Scheme,unimplemented-surface-scheme,"Selecting `gemini` before the Gemini provider exists is rejected at configuration load rather than failing at request time."
  EC-010,Non-ASCII Name,non-ascii-name,"A canonical name containing non-ASCII characters is sanitized into the scheme's allowed set, decoded through the map, and treated as lossy only if truncation or a hash suffix is required."
